cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite the associated manuscript."
title: "GALAXI: Scalable machine learning framework for multiphase identification from powder X-ray diffraction"
version: "0.1.0"
authors:
  - family-names: Tong
    given-names: Xinyang
  - family-names: Jin
    given-names: Ethan
  - family-names: Xu
    given-names: Jiahan
  - family-names: Rao
    given-names: Aditya
  - family-names: Jiang
    given-names: Pengcen
  - family-names: Szymanski
    given-names: Nathan J.
repository-code: "https://github.com/Szymanski-Group/galaxi"
abstract: |
  X-ray diffraction (XRD) is the primary tool for identifying crystalline
  phases after synthesis, but automated identification remains difficult
  for realistic, multi-phase samples. GALAXI (General Automated Learning
  and Analysis for XRD-based Identification) decouples phase
  identification into independent, phase-specific one-versus-all binary
  classifiers that each specialize in recognizing a single phase. These
  pre-trained classifiers first narrow the search space to a small set of
  plausible phases, which are then evaluated by automated Rietveld
  refinement to identify the combination that best explains the full
  diffraction pattern.
